  "title": "Tektronix 7854 Waveform Processing Oscilloscope",
  "year": "1980",
  "subtitle": "A 400 MHz oscilloscope with a detachable RPN calculator keyboard — the lab instrument as programmable computer",
  "why_scout_cares": "A collision of two interface paradigms in one 42-pound instrument. The engineer uses traditional analog knobs for signal viewing AND a detachable calculator-style keyboard for waveform mathematics — adding, multiplying, taking FFTs, computing logs — in Reverse Polish Notation, like an HP-41C bolted to the side of a premium oscilloscope. Macros could be bound to physical function keys. The keyboard was physically detachable, acknowledging that an oscilloscope must transform between two completely distinct operational modes. This was not a computer with a scope card added; this was a scope that had been taught to compute. The same TMS9900 CPU that powered the TI-99/4 home computer ran the waveform processor. Fills a gap: no scientific instruments in the museum's 140 exhibits, and no device where the hybrid analog/digital interface collision is the entire point.",
  "search_terms": ["Tektronix 7854 waveform processing oscilloscope", "detachable RPN keyboard oscilloscope", "Tek 7854 smart scope"],
